00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:24.654
in general.

00:06:24.654 --> 00:06:27.070
And then we're going to jump
into doing this lab together,

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.903
and you guys can time
to see if we can do it

00:06:28.903 --> 00:06:31.280
in 10 minutes or less.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.780
All right, one of
my favorite topics

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.070
to talk about-- before
my career here at Google,

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.730
I was a freelance web
designer and web developer,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.169
and I ended up wearing
a ton of different hats.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:41.960
So although I really
like to work, and make

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.460
these beautiful
graphics and images,

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.650
and design a beautiful
UI for the site,

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:48.290
ultimately it came time to
answer the question of where

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:49.370
am I going to host this?

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.050
Where am I going
to store this data?

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.030
If folks are uploading
things to my site,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.990
where are those ultimately
going to get stored?

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.600
So in addition to
having a design hat

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.340
on-- or if you're writing
software programs,

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.923
having your programming head on,
which is what you really want

00:07:00.923 --> 00:07:01.760
to focus on--

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.280
back in the day, you
also had to be almost

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.980
like a hardware engineer or
an infrastructure engineer

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:09.620
just to make sure that you
had enough storage for all

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.895
the things that you're
looking to store and serve.

00:07:11.895 --> 00:07:14.520
You had enough compute power if
you're running a lot of compute

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.970
and computational jobs--
or if you just had any kind

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.634
of computations for
your applications,

00:07:18.634 --> 00:07:21.050
you needed to make sure that
you had a big enough server--

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.460
a CPU to process that--

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.850
also for latency-- if you
have enough networking.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.850
And last but not least, if a
server disk fails, or there is

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:30.470
an operating system update
that needs to be installed,

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:34.039
you also need to be kind of
a DBA or an infrastructure

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:36.080
technician to make sure
that all of your hardware

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.350
is running smoothly.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:39.058
So you wore many of
these different hats,

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:41.210
and we all know jack of
all trades, master of none.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.736
It's not in your best interest
to be focused on infrastructure

00:07:44.736 --> 00:07:47.360
when, ultimately, you want to be
focused on building the latest

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.370
and greatest business
software-- whatever

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.420
it is that your business
is looking to do.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:57.200
So what happened in
the last 10 years?

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.840
So back when I was
building web sites,

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:03.060
I had to buy or rent
out a box server, that's

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.740
sitting in my closet somewhere,
to ultimately set up these web

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.350
pages, and I have to go
on and manually install

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:10.360
software updates and
operating system updates.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.406
Where has that space gone?

00:08:12.406 --> 00:08:13.780
So all on the way
on the left you

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.680
see there is the data
center, where I'm

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.110
managing all this stuff myself.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.080
We call it on-premise,
or physical,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.950
or you're co-located there.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.262
And eventually it
moved into, well,

00:08:23.262 --> 00:08:24.970
everyone else is having
the same problem.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.511
We all don't want
to buy these boxes.

00:08:26.511 --> 00:08:28.734
And even if we're perfectly
good at maintaining them,

00:08:28.734 --> 00:08:31.150
and we're experts at hardware,
a lot of times the machines

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:31.960
are sitting unused.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.793
And we're still paying
for electricity costs

00:08:33.793 --> 00:08:35.590
even when the demand
on, say, our website

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.470
isn't that high until
Black Friday comes along,

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.451
and then coincidentally we don't
have enough server capacity.

00:08:41.451 --> 00:08:43.659
So it's hard to manage the
ups and downs when we just

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.496
have a static
amount of hardware,

00:08:45.496 --> 00:08:46.870
so what that
generally shifted to

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:48.730
was a virtualization
environment, where

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.870
we could rent out time
in these machines.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.411
But you would still be
responsible for installing,

00:08:53.411 --> 00:08:55.910
and managing, and dealing with
all the maintenance headaches

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.010
that went therein as well.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.300
So what has ultimately happened
with this big push to Cloud?

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.420
So all the way on the right,
you see that the catchphrase

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.710
is that it's serverless.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.539
It's fully automated, so unless
you really liked managing that

00:09:08.539 --> 00:09:11.080
hardware yourself, which a lot
of people didn't-- they really

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.100
wanted to focus on
their core competency,

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.950
which is building
amazing software models,

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:18.019
designing incredible web site
and application experiences--

00:09:18.019 --> 00:09:20.060
you no longer need to
worry about infrastructure.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:21.040
It's fully managed.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.036
And in the example that I
gave, where Black Friday you

00:09:24.036 --> 00:09:25.660
might not have enough
servers, and then

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.780
if there is a slow
Monday or Tuesday

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:30.260
you have way too much capacity.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:32.800
So you are wasting time, and
you have the opportunity cost

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:33.970
in servers sitting there.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.820
You want this more of an elastic
scalable when you need it,

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:38.470
and it scales down
when you don't.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:42.950
And that's exactly where Google
Cloud Platform comes into play.

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:44.806
So Google Cloud Platform--

00:09:44.806 --> 00:09:46.180
I like to say
Google's been doing

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.660
Cloud for more than 10
years, but you've just

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:50.930
heard of it recently.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:54.400
That's because Google has been
running its own private Cloud,

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.280
because it ultimately had to
solve the exact same problems

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.130
that you've had
to solve, which is

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:01.630
if you're going to be organizing
all the world's information

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.200
and making it accessible to
everybody around the world,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.550
Google had to solve this
hardware problem for itself.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:10.420
And so, they invested millions
of dollars in infrastructure,

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:11.920
and building these
undersea cables

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.697
that you see here
for networking,

00:10:13.697 --> 00:10:15.530
and these data centers
all around the world,

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.300
including Edge locations to make
sure that Google's web pages

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.220
and search queries can
be accessed anywhere

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:22.490
throughout the world.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.030
So as they like to say,
don't reinvent the wheel.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:27.460
If Google's already got the
infrastructure in place,

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:31.180
and it's good enough for things
like Gmail, Google Photos,

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:32.860
and any of the other
Google applications

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.350
in answering your
search queries,

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.820
it's going to be good enough
for your applications as well.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.960
So this is ultimately
what makes Google Google.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.820
And only recently in
the last five years

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:46.660
or so, Google has made that
hardware, that infrastructure,

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:49.521
available to you all for your
use in what's called the Google

00:10:49.521 --> 00:10:50.145
Cloud Platform.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.730
So say I've convinced
